{"word": "greed", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "greed", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɡɹiːd/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "greeds", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "A selfish or excessive desire for more than is needed or deserved, especially of money, wealth, food, or other possessions.", "tags": ["uncountable", "usually"], "examples": ["His greed was his undoing."], "quotes": [{"text": "[…]But when I had bestridden the plank, quoth I to myself, \"Thou deserveth all that betideth thee. All this is decreed to me of Allah (whose name be exalted!), to turn me from my greed of gain, whence ariseth all that I endure, for I have wealth galore.\"", "reference": "1885, Richard F. Burton, The Book of the Thousand Nights and a Night, Night 564:"}, {"text": "Your market gardener is not a well-breeched man, dependant as he is on the imponderables of glut, the inequities of distribution, and the greeds of wholesaler and retailer.", "reference": "1954, Thomas Firbank, Log Hut, page 194:"}, {"text": "She does not display voracities and greeds like a number of patients […] nor has she shown any significant tendency to possessiveness, grievance, paranoia, or mania.", "reference": "1973, Oliver Sacks, Awakenings:"}, {"text": "The point is, ladies and gentleman, that greed, for lack of a better word, is good. Greed is right, greed works. Greed clarifies, cuts through, and captures the essence of the evolutionary spirit. Greed, in all of its forms; greed for life, for money, for love, knowledge has marked the upward surge of mankind.", "reference": "1987, Stanley Weiser, Oliver Stone, Wall Street (motion picture), spoken by Gordon Gekko (Michael Douglas):"}], "synonyms": ["avarice", "covetousness", "greediness", "rapacity", "gluttony"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "sq", "name": "Albanian"}, "word": "lakmi"}, {"language":